cros_ec: poll the EC event FIFO when IRQ/notify never fires (Pixel Slate fix)

On the Google Pixel Slate (nocturne, x86 LPC Chrome EC) the EC never
delivers MKBP events to the AP. Confirmed on-device by three independent
measurements, all with the EC's own console proving the events happen:

  - EC console logs every button press AND "MKBP common FIFO depth 16
    reached" (the EC's outgoing event FIFO overflowing), yet:
  - the hardware IRQ (chromeos-ec, IRQ 83) shows a total count of 1 for the
    whole boot -- it fires once (the boot-time unlock drain in
    cros_ec_register) and never again;
  - /sys/firmware/acpi/interrupts: no GPE and no SCI movement on button press;
  - /sys/kernel/debug/wakeup_sources: zero movement on an isolated 5-press
    test.

The DSDT confirms the button ACPI node (GOOG0007, "EC MKBP Device") has no
_PRW/_CRS/interrupt resource of its own -- only the parent GOOG0004 does, and
its wake GPE (gpe70) is disabled. So neither the threaded IRQ path
(cros_ec.c) nor the ACPI-notify path (cros_ec_lpc.c, Notify 0x80 on GOOG0004)
ever drains the FIFO after boot. The result: volume buttons, the sensor FIFO
(auto-rotate), and lid-angle are all permanently silent.

The synchronous EC command path is unaffected -- cros_ec_get_next_event()
drains the FIFO fine on demand, which is exactly what the existing boot-time
and resume-time drains rely on. This patch adds an optional periodic drain
(the same cros_ec_handle_event() loop the IRQ thread runs) as a fallback for
such boards.

Design:
  - Off by default. A module param, cros_ec.ec_event_poll_ms, controls it:
    -1 = auto (enable per a DMI allow-list; currently Google Nocturne),
     0 = off, >0 = poll period in milliseconds (default 40 when auto).
  - Runs from a delayed_work (process context) because cros_ec_get_next_event()
    issues a sleeping EC command; a timer/softirq could not.
  - Paused across suspend (cancel in the suspend-late disable path) and
    resumed in the resume-early enable path, so no EC commands are issued
    while the EC is down.
  - Zero effect on boards where the async path works: the work is initialised
    but never scheduled unless the board is on the allow-list (or the param is
    set), and the extra struct field is inert otherwise.

If the async EC event delivery is ever root-caused and fixed at the
firmware/ACPI level, set cros_ec.ec_event_poll_ms=0 to disable this with no
rebuild.

Signed-off-by: BobZKernel

